When content changes, dispatch is triggered from `server/router/api/v1/memo_service_webhooks.go` via `webhook.PostAsync`, which enqueues payloads for background workers. Those workers call **`Post`**, where the outbound HTTP actually runs through `safeClient.Do(req)`:

```119:152:internal/webhook/webhook.go
// Post posts the message to webhook endpoint.
func Post(requestPayload *WebhookRequestPayload) error {
	// ...
	resp, err := safeClient.Do(req)
```

`safeClient` uses a custom transport whose **`safeDialContext`** resolves the target hostname and rejects any address in a reserved/private range before dialing, which blocks SSRF and DNS rebinding at connection time:

```29:76:internal/webhook/webhook.go
	// safeClient is the shared HTTP client used for all webhook dispatches.
	// Its Transport guards against SSRF by blocking connections to reserved/private
	// IP addresses at dial time, which also defeats DNS rebinding attacks.
	safeClient = &http.Client{
		Timeout: timeout,
		Transport: &http.Transport{
			DialContext: safeDialContext,
		},
	}
// ...
func safeDialContext(ctx context.Context, network, addr string) (net.Conn, error) {
	// ...
		if ip := net.ParseIP(ipStr); ip != nil && isReservedIP(ip) {
			return nil, errors.Errorf("webhook: connection to reserved/private IP address is not allowed")
		}
```

There is also registration-time checking: **`ValidateURL`** in `internal/webhook/validate.go` uses the same `isReservedIP` logic against `reservedCIDRs` when webhooks are created or updated (`server/router/api/v1/user_service_webhooks.go`). That can be bypassed at runtime via the `--allow-private-webhooks` flag (`cmd/memos/main.go`), which sets `webhook.AllowPrivateIPs`.
